    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d411">

                <pb facs="0628" n="595" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v23/pg595"/>

                <head>411. Memorandum From Robert W. Komer of the National Security Council Staff to
                    the President’s Special Assistant for National Security Affairs (Bundy)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d411-f1"/></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1965-03-26">March 26,
                    1965</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <opener>
                    <salute>McGB:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>On our first go-round Ball vetoed the idea of our contributing [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">dollar amount not declassified</hi>] to Tshombe’s
                    campaign fund. He’s afraid of publicity. So I agreed to let the matter ride
                    awhile.</p>
                <p>But Tshombe has brought it up again with Godley,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d411-f2"/>
                    which makes it an issue of confidence. So we’re going back to Ball with
                    following arguments for going ahead: (1) We’re already doing so much in the
                    Congo that’s obvious (planes, military mission) that a few rumors about
                    political meddling won’t add noticeably to the din; (2) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >This is the only handle we have for getting Tshombe not to angle for
                        Kasavubu’s job</hi>. Africans are beginning to accept the Kasavubu–Tshombe
                    team; if Tshombe knocked off his mantle of legitimacy, it would cost us plenty;
                    (3) Tshombe will surely read a turndown as a sign that we’re backing away from
                    him.</p>
                <p>After making sure we can handle the transfer without leaving visible tracks, I
                    plan to argue the case again with Ball tomorrow. I’d like to use your proxy
                        again.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d411-f3"/></p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>RWK</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d411-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d411-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Johnson Library, National Security File, Country File, Congo, Vol. XI,
                    Memos &amp; Miscellaneous, 1/65-9/65. Secret.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d411-f2" n="2" xml:id="d411-f2-content">
                <p>On March 24, the Leopoldville Station transmitted a message from Godley to
                    McIlvaine, reiterating Tshombe’s request for U.S. financial assistance and
                    quoting Tshombe’s assurances that he was collaborating closely with Kasavubu who
                    had no objection to such assistance. The Ambassador thus recommended that [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">text not declassified</hi>] be made available to CONACO.
                    (Central Intelligence Agency Files, [<hi rendition="#italic">text not
                        declassified</hi>], Fiche 44, Row 4, Frames 2–5, [<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >text not declassified</hi>])</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d411-f3" n="3" xml:id="d411-f3-content">
                <p>A handwritten notation in the margin of the original reads: “OK.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
